# T18-02A — Final Semantic and API Clarifications (Plan Mode)

The current revision resolves the four architectural issues from the previous review. Do not rewrite the entire plan.

Resolve the following remaining issues with repository evidence.

## 1. MERGED_FROM grouping semantics — critical

The plan groups candidates by `(child, relation)` but currently populates `parent_version_ids` only with unresolved parents.

This may violate the minimum parent cardinality of `MERGED_FROM`.

Analyze this concrete graph:

- child has two `MERGED_FROM` parents;
- parent A exists in `graph.nodes`;
- parent B does not exist in `graph.nodes`;
- therefore exactly one parent is unresolved.

Determine the normative meaning of `ContinuityAssessment.parent_version_ids` under CORE-001 §6.2.1 and the relevant relation constraints.

Compare:

A. Assessment contains only unresolved parents.

B. Assessment contains all declared parents for the `(child, relation)` group, while contextual validation establishes that at least one parent is unresolved.

C. A different representation justified by the existing specification.

For each option, explain:
- whether relation cardinality is preserved;
- whether the Assessment accurately represents the declaration;
- how `PARENT_UNRESOLVED` is validated;
- whether resolved parents can legitimately appear in the Assessment.

Do not infer normative semantics from implementation convenience.

Recommend one model and define the exact contextual validation rules.

Add test cases for:
- all parents unresolved;
- one resolved and one unresolved MERGED_FROM parent;
- all parents resolved;
- multiple relations involving the same parent.

If CORE-001 is ambiguous, keep the question OPEN and identify whether a separate spec clarification is required.

## 2. Registry validation precondition

The plan states that Registry descriptors are caller-validated, but `buildLineageGraph` does not run `validateDescriptor`.

Inspect:
- `Registry` type and contract;
- all current callers of `buildLineageGraph`;
- all current uses of `validateDescriptor`;
- whether a caller can pass an unvalidated or contradictory Registry;
- whether duplicate version IDs with divergent lineage can produce a builder-issued graph.

Compare:

A. Runtime validation in `buildLineageGraph`.

B. A separate validated-Registry boundary/type.

C. Explicit caller precondition with documented enforcement at all current call sites.

Do not automatically add validation if it would change the intentionally structural behavior of graph construction.

Distinguish graph construction semantics from descriptor validation semantics. Recommend the smallest safe approach for T18-02A and state any remaining limitation.

## 3. Reference adapter public API status

The plan exports `emitOrphanAssessment` from the package root to satisfy the AEBE package-root criterion.

Clarify whether this is:
- a stable public API;
- an explicitly experimental/reference API;
- or a temporary conformance adapter.

Inspect package API conventions and roadmap wording.

Specify the compatibility commitment and whether the roadmap criterion can be met by exercising the public Core API while keeping the adapter internal.

Do not silently make a reference adapter a permanent protocol API.

## 4. Atomicity wording

Preserve the batch all-or-nothing contract for Core admission and returned immutable representation.

Explicitly state that arbitrary external side effects performed by `assessmentIdFor` cannot be rolled back by Core.

Specify:
- callbacks run before Core admission;
- callback failure means Core admission is never invoked;
- Core admission failure returns no new registered representation;
- caller-managed persistence occurs only after complete success.

Do not claim transactionality over external side effects.

## 5. Output

Return only:
- evidence and findings for these four questions;
- exact changes to the implementation plan;
- remaining project-owner decisions;
- final gate.

No files, source, tests, specifications or documentation may be modified. No commits or PRs.

